<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Noli</h1><p class="st-meta">1192 CE – 1797 CE · capital Noli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Ligurian seaside republic that kept its independence, under Genoese protection, from 1192 until 1797.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Noli, a small port on the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-riviera">Riviera</a>, freed itself from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/del-carretto">Del Carretto</a> marquises and became a self-governing commune in 1192. It soon placed itself under the protection of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa</a> while keeping its own institutions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Noli was governed by consuls and later by a council and podestà, and lived from fishing, shipbuilding and sea trade. Its ships joined Genoese expeditions, including crusading voyages. In 1239 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> made it a bishopric, a sign of its standing. The town kept its medieval walls, towers and the Romanesque church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-paragorio">San Paragorio</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Noli survived as a nominal republic allied to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> for six centuries. It was absorbed into the French-backed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ligurian-republic">Ligurian Republic</a> in 1797.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-noli.jpg" alt="Republic of Noli" width="320" height="234"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Noli.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1192 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Noli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ligurian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt>